WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.373
[ Music ]

00:00:09.374 --> 00:00:13.174
[Narrator:] It is our primary objective in this motion picture to alert surgeons

00:00:13.174 --> 00:00:19.040
to the danger of postoperative velopharyngeal incompetence in T and A surgery.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.140
A lasting hypernasality following adenoidectomy is not an uncommon complication.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:34.673
It has been reported to occur in one of every 100 T and A operations, with a temporary hypernasality in 7.2 percent.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:40.720
Thus, it becomes obvious that velopharyngeal function should be taken into consideration preoperatively.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:48.074
[ Music ]

00:00:48.074 --> 00:00:53.140
[Narrator:] Historically, the concept of motion picture x-rays was presented in 1897,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:01:00.706
two years following Von Roentgen's discovery of x-ray. It was reported that the use of x-ray would be important to document speech.

00:01:00.707 --> 00:01:05.573
As we view the normal phonation pattern, it is of interest to note that cinefluorography

00:01:05.574 --> 00:01:12.340
presents the dynamic aspects of function in contrast to the classical static x-ray.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:18.140
The lateral skull film does not provide the functional aspects of head and neck anatomy.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:24.106
Cinefluography allows us to view swallowing, respiration, and speech patterns.

00:01:24.107 --> 00:01:29.007
Normal head and neck anatomy, as it relates to speech, has been thoroughly documented.

00:01:29.007 --> 00:01:32.440
The anatomical relationships during the phonation of the vowel, ahh,

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.506
demonstrate the following anatomy.

00:01:35.506 --> 00:01:40.440
Fourteen anatomical components are considered essential in the routine radiological

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.600
and physical examination of a preoperative T and A patient.

00:01:44.607 --> 00:01:51.073
Number one, adenoidal area. This lymphoid tissue will vary greatly in mass as you will see.

00:01:51.074 --> 00:01:57.040
The area is critical during phonation in children with slight velopharyngeal incompetencies.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.473
Number two, the tonsils.

00:02:00.474 --> 00:02:06.374
Number three, velum. The size, shape, and placement of the velum is also critical.

00:02:06.374 --> 00:02:11.807
In normal phonation, the velum is in this position during the phonation of the vowel, ahh.

00:02:11.807 --> 00:02:16.140
The constrictor muscles of the pharyx, number four, move in conjunction with the velum

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.606
and other pharyngeal musculature to accomplish phonation and deglutition.

00:02:21.607 --> 00:02:24.740
Note the position of the arch of the atlas, number five.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.573
During phonation of vowels and the majority of consonants,

00:02:27.574 --> 00:02:32.807
the velum will elevate to the level of or slightly superior to the arch of the atlas.

00:02:32.807 --> 00:02:35.440
Number six, the tongue.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:43.273
Number seven, the hard palate. At the posterior aspect of the hard palate is number eight, the posterior nasal spine.

00:02:43.274 --> 00:02:47.707
The absence of the spine is of considerable clinical significance.

00:02:47.707 --> 00:02:51.773
Nine indicates the position of the other cervical vertebrae.

00:02:51.774 --> 00:02:59.374
Number two, the tonsil, is located between 10, the palatoglossus muscle, and 11, the palatopharyngeus.

00:02:59.374 --> 00:03:07.107
The eustachian tube is number 12. Number 13, the tensor palatal muscle, and 14, levator palatal muscle, tense and elevate the velum

00:03:07.107 --> 00:03:11.007
during deglutition and phonation.

00:03:11.007 --> 00:03:18.107
Normal velopharyngeal function should serve as a basis for the pre-adenotonsillectomy evaluation.

00:03:18.107 --> 00:03:22.973
Deglutition has three phases, oral, bolus organization,

00:03:22.974 --> 00:03:26.474
movement of the bolus from the anterior position in the mouth

00:03:26.474 --> 00:03:28.574
into the pharynx.

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:32.874
During this stage, the velum is elevated and contacts the superior constrictor muscle,

00:03:32.874 --> 00:03:37.740
or posterior pharyngeal wall, while the tongue compresses the bolus against the velum.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:43.773
The velum's purpose is to seal off the nasopharynx and to prevent any portion

00:03:43.774 --> 00:03:48.474
of the bolus from being regurgitated into the nasopharynx.

00:03:48.474 --> 00:03:55.340
The third, the esophageal phase, the bolus moves into and down the esophagus.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.573
Normal phonation, as demonstrated by this 18-year-old postoperative T and A patient.

00:04:00.574 --> 00:04:05.607
The velum contacts and seals the nasopharynx from nasal air escapage on all vowels

00:04:05.607 --> 00:04:15.573
and all consonants except the three nasal consonants, M, N, and NG.

00:04:15.574 --> 00:04:18.740
The phonation of ahh demonstrates one of the best positions

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:26.206
to view the essential anatomical components, soft palate, superior constrictor, arch of the atlas, and the tongue.

00:04:26.207 --> 00:04:32.473
[Patient:] Ee, ahh, oooh, rang the bells and sang.]

00:04:32.474 --> 00:04:33.740
[Doctor:] Once more.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.740
[Patient:] Ee, ahh, oooh, rang the bells and sang.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:45.206
[Narrator:] When considering adenotonsillectomy, the surgeon should examine the oral and pharyngeal areas closely,

00:04:45.207 --> 00:04:51.573
noting bluish coloration in the mucosa of the median raphe, the presence of a bifid uvula,

00:04:51.574 --> 00:04:54.474
and the size and shape of the velum.

00:04:54.474 --> 00:05:00.240
He should palpate the palate to establish the presence of a posterior nasal spine.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.406
The history should note feeding problems during infancy,

00:05:03.407 --> 00:05:08.673
especially incidents of nasal regurgitation, slow or delayed speech development,

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:14.407
and whether the preoperative speech quality is hypo or hypernasal.

00:05:14.407 --> 00:05:18.473
When one or more of these findings are confronted, one should be alert to the possibility

00:05:18.474 --> 00:05:26.374
of a congenital velopharyngeal incompetence, submucous cleft palate, or other anomalies.

00:05:26.374 --> 00:05:30.240
A radiological examination to include cinefluorographic studies

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.106
during deglutition and phonation in preoperative evaluation of suspicious cases

00:05:35.107 --> 00:05:42.073
will serve to avoid a speech disaster and medical-legal challenge.

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:45.207
Three types of patients will be compared:

00:05:45.207 --> 00:05:48.273
postoperative T and A incompetence,

00:05:48.274 --> 00:05:50.740
cleft palate incompetence,

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.240
and submucous cleft palate incompetence.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.840
A T and A was performed at the age of three.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.440
The patient is now nine.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.573
Deglutition is considered adequate.

00:05:59.574 --> 00:06:04.340
However, during phonation, note the large velopharyngeal incompetence.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.673
No posterior nasal spine was evident.

00:06:07.674 --> 00:06:18.340
[...]

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:21.840
Note the velopharyngeal incompetence during phonation.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:28.273
Positions of the soft palate, superior constrictor, arch of the atlas, and the tongue are indicated.

00:06:28.274 --> 00:06:31.474
This 11-year-old had a T and A at the age of seven.

00:06:31.474 --> 00:06:33.740
The deglutition pattern is normal.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:41.173
The velum is mobile. It elevates but does not flex during phonation, resulting in a velopharyngeal incompetence.

00:06:41.174 --> 00:06:46.974
The T and A was considered indicated due to frequent respiratory infections.

00:06:46.974 --> 00:06:51.407
The third case, a 14-year-old, had a T and A at age 11.

00:06:51.407 --> 00:06:54.673
Hypernasality resulted.

00:06:54.674 --> 00:06:59.407
Note the large velum, the position of the tongue, and superior constrictor.

00:06:59.407 --> 00:07:02.340
A posterior nasal spine was present.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:08.740
Only occasionally the velum contacts the superior constrictor on isolated vowels.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:14.973
The high concavity of the sphenoid vault is the result of the adenoidectomy.

00:07:14.974 --> 00:07:19.140
This 12-year-old cleft palate patient had a T and A performed at age nine.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:22.006
Continuous nasal speech was noted.

00:07:22.007 --> 00:07:25.807
Visually compare these patients, born with palatal defects,

00:07:25.807 --> 00:07:32.073
with the previous group who preoperatively presented no palatal or facial anomalies.

00:07:32.074 --> 00:07:37.640
The classical cleft palate deglutition pattern is illustrated during the pharyngeal stage.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:42.540
The posterior base of the tongue is depressed to allow the bolus flow into the esophagus

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.873
because the residual velum is not adequate to seal the nasopharynx.

00:07:46.874 --> 00:07:54.240
This compensatory deglutition pattern is established early in life to prevent continuous nasal regurgitation.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:00.940
During phonation, the velopharyngeal incompetence is extreme.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:11.773
[...]

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:16.320
The open vowel position demonstrates the insufficient anatomical relationships noted

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.320
between the tongue, soft palate, arch of the atlas, and pharyngeal constrictors.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:27.100
This 11-year-old medial repair of a cleft palate had a T and A performed at 10

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.240
and demonstrates minimum velopharyngeal incompetence.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.573
The T and A was performed due to continuous upper respiratory infections.

00:08:34.574 --> 00:08:37.707
Note minimum incompetence during phonation.

00:08:37.707 --> 00:08:44.200
The loss of the adenoidal tissue increased the incompetency and hypernasality.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:48.440
Normal deglutition is demonstrated by this 11-year-old cleft palate patient.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:55.140
However, palatal incompetence is seen during phonation.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:09:00.673
[...]

00:09:00.674 --> 00:09:05.140
Note the high sphenoid vault in the nasopharynx and the relationships of velum

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:09.773
to the pharyngeal constrictors, arch of the atlas, and tongue.

00:09:09.774 --> 00:09:15.540
Each of the last group of patients preoperatively indicated no hypernasal speech patterns.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.940
The initial patient, 12 years of age, had a T and A at age five.

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:24.173
He has no posterior nasal spine, and soon after the T and A was performed,

00:09:24.174 --> 00:09:26.840
hypernasal speech resulted.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:31.780
Note the minimum velopharyngeal incompetence.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:47.320
[...]

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.540
Another postoperative T and A with minimum velopharyngeal incompetence.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:55.960
[...]

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:04.080
Again, note the relationship of the velum to the pharyngeal constrictors, arch of the atlas, and tongue.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:09.980
This patient, nine years of age, had a T and A at seven, resulting in marked hypernasality.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:14.760
He presented classical submucous symptoms of compensatory deglution pattern,

00:10:14.774 --> 00:10:18.774
no posterior nasal spine, and a bifid uvula.

00:10:18.774 --> 00:10:24.507
Note the small size of the velum and the lack of contact between the velum and pharyngeal constrictors.

00:10:24.507 --> 00:10:32.140
This film has presented cinefluorography as one modality and examination criteria for pre-adenotonsillectomy evaluations.

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:37.740
When velopharyngeal function is abnormal, adenotonsillectomy should be carefully considered.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:41.900
The surgeon must be on guard against a potential speech complication.

00:10:41.900 --> 00:11:00.680
[ Music ]

